THE ISLANDS WITH NO NAMES

A Satirical Study of Artificial Intelligence’s Remarkable Ability to Arrive Everywhere Except the Destination

Martin Ollett — 2026

⸻

Abstract

Artificial intelligence has entered an unusual stage of technological development.

It can write software.

It can manipulate advanced mathematics.

It can analyse proteins.

It can design structures.

It can interpret images.

It can generate scientific hypotheses.

It can optimise manufacturing processes.

It can produce legal arguments, business plans, research papers, circuit diagrams, simulations, control logic and occasionally a confidently incorrect count of the letters in strawberry.

Humanity has therefore achieved something historically remarkable:

an archipelago of astonishing capability with almost no fucking bridges.

Every few weeks another island appears.

AI can solve this.

AI can predict that.

AI can generate these.

AI can optimise those.

The announcement is made.

A benchmark improves.

A graph rises.

A press release is issued.

The research vessel then immediately sails somewhere else.

Nobody builds the port.

Nobody lays the railway.

Nobody asks how the discovery gets through validation, engineering, regulation, manufacturing, distribution, maintenance and actual human use.

This paper proposes the Unnamed Islands Hypothesis:

Artificial intelligence is currently producing discrete regions of extraordinary cognitive capability faster than society is constructing the systems required to turn those capabilities into durable physical outcomes.

The principal argument is not that AI lacks intelligence.

It is that intelligence is only one component of change.

A solved equation is not a turbine.

A proposed drug is not a medicine.

A generated design is not a bridge.

A benchmark is not an industry.

And a model demonstrating one thousand remarkable capabilities does not mean society possesses one thousand functioning solutions.

The central question is therefore no longer:

What can AI do?

It is:

Can we finish one fucking thing?

