4. The Fundamental Conversion Error

The artificial-intelligence discussion frequently performs the following conversion:

\text{AI knows how}

therefore

\text{humanity now has}

This conversion is unsupported.

Consider the statements:

We know how to build a fusion reactor.

and

There is a commercially operating fusion power station supplying my kettle.

These are not the same sentence.

Similarly:

An AI has generated a potentially effective molecule.

is not synonymous with:

Your doctor can prescribe it tomorrow morning.

Between the two sits what this paper identifies as:

The Fucking Middle

The Fucking Middle contains nearly everything required to make civilisation work.

⸻

5. Proof ≠ Product

Suppose an AI provides a revolutionary theorem concerning fluid dynamics.

It is correct.

Mathematicians verify it.

History is made.

Marvelous.

Now take the theorem to an engineer designing a turbine.

The engineer asks:

“Can I put this into the production CFD workflow?”

No.

“Does it produce a validated numerical scheme?”

Not yet.

“Has that scheme been implemented?”

No.

“Benchmarked?”

No.

“Compared with experimental data?”

No.

“Certified?”

No.

“Can I use it on the engine?”

Absolutely fucking not.

But mathematically:

SOLVED.

This is the Theorem-to-Turbine Gap.

It is the distance between:

\boxed{\text{we know something}}

and

\boxed{\text{we can reliably do something with it}}.

The distance is frequently several industries wide.

