6. The Billion-Einstein Problem

One popular conception of superintelligence imagines effectively unlimited research capability.

Suppose we grant it.

Tomorrow morning humanity receives the equivalent intellectual output of:

1,000,000,000\ \text{Einsteins}.

Excellent.

At 09:00 they produce ten million experimental proposals.

At 09:01 humanity discovers it does not possess ten million laboratories.

By lunchtime:

\text{ideas/hour}
\gg
\text{experiments/hour}.

The bottleneck moves.

Build more laboratories.

Excellent.

Now:

\text{experiments/hour}
\gg
\text{specialist technicians/hour}.

Train technicians.

Excellent.

Now:

\text{validated discoveries/hour}
\gg
\text{manufacturing capacity/hour}.

Build factories.

Excellent.

Factories require machines.

Machines require factories.

Factories require skilled people.

Skilled people require training.

Training requires time.

The omnipotent intelligence has discovered an ancient and formidable adversary:

Lead Time.

