9. The Great Mission Proposal

Instead of celebrating one thousand disconnected demonstrations, this paper proposes an alternative experiment.

Pick one major problem.

Not twenty.

Not one hundred.

One.

For example:

Can AI materially accelerate development of a new curative HIV intervention from research question to deployed treatment?

Then do not declare victory when:

* a molecule is suggested;
* a paper is written;
* a simulation succeeds;
* a benchmark improves;
* a laboratory result looks promising.

Victory occurs when the thing reaches its intended recipient safely and reproducibly.

The entire pipeline becomes the experiment:

\text{research}
\rightarrow
\text{design}
\rightarrow
\text{experiment}
\rightarrow
\text{validation}
\rightarrow
\text{production}
\rightarrow
\text{approval}
\rightarrow
\text{distribution}
\rightarrow
\text{use}.

Every bottleneck is documented.

Every human intervention is recorded.

Every failure is classified.

Every missing skill is identified.

Every infrastructure deficiency becomes visible.

Then something genuinely valuable happens.

Humanity learns not merely whether AI can solve a scientific problem.

Humanity learns how to turn AI-generated intelligence into physical change.

