17. Discussion: Perhaps Intelligence Was Never the Whole Bottleneck

There is an uncomfortable possibility.

Human civilisation may already possess vastly more knowledge than it has implementation capacity.

We know how to improve buildings.

We know how to improve transport.

We know how to improve public health.

We know how to generate energy.

We know how to manufacture extraordinary machines.

Yet implementation remains slow because knowledge is only one variable.

The actual system resembles:

\text{Outcome}
=
f(
\text{knowledge},
\text{capital},
\text{labour},
\text{equipment},
\text{institutions},
\text{time},
\text{materials},
\text{coordination}
).

AI can push the knowledge variable extremely hard.

The others do not automatically follow.

An infinite number multiplied by zero remains inconvenient.

