19. Conclusion

Artificial intelligence is not presently suffering from a shortage of impressive demonstrations.

It is suffering from a shortage of completed journeys.

The industry continually demonstrates:

AI can begin this.

AI can assist that.

AI can discover these.

AI can design those.

Good.

Now finish one.

Take one genuinely important human problem.

Follow it from ignorance to discovery.

From discovery to experiment.

From experiment to verification.

From verification to engineering.

From engineering to production.

From production to deployment.

From deployment to measurable improvement in human life.

Record every failure.

Build every missing interface.

Train every missing specialist.

Construct the infrastructure.

Standardise the handoffs.

Then do it again.

Because perhaps the most important artificial-intelligence benchmark was never:

\text{How intelligent is the machine?}

Perhaps it is:

\boxed{\text{Can intelligence survive contact with civilisation?}}

Until then, we remain explorers standing on beaches.

Behind us sits an extraordinary island containing mathematics, chemistry, medicine, engineering and software that would have appeared supernatural twenty years ago.

Ahead sits another announcement.

Someone points toward the horizon.

LAND!

Everyone gets back in the fucking boat.

The island still has no name.
